## Stream — pull-based, backpressured async stream (spec §4.5). The async
## analogue of `Enumerable[T]`: defined over ONE required primitive
## `def var poll_next(cx) -> Poll[Option[Int]]`, with `for_each`/`count`/`map`/
## `filter`/`take` as DEFAULT BODIES built on `poll_next` — exactly how
## `Enumerable[T]` is defined over `each`. Backpressure is intrinsic to PULL
## semantics: the consumer drives `poll_next`, so upstream is polled only when
## downstream demands. Resource safety from ownership + `def drop`: a source
## stream owns its reactor registration / `state_ptr` and tears it down at scope
## exit (including mid-stream cancellation — the consumer stops pulling, drops
## the stream).
##
## v1 surface (what the current lowering supports):
##   * `mixin Stream dispatch runtime` — `poll_next` dispatches through the
##     runtime vtable (`Stream_dynamic_poll_next`), so adapters can hold a
##     heterogeneous inner stream by value and poll it polymorphically. (Same
##     `dispatch runtime` mechanism as `Future`; the combination of a
##     runtime-dispatch mixin WITH default-body methods is new here and works.)
##   * **`for_each` is a SYNCHRONOUS pump driver** — it builds a `Context`,
##     pumps `poll_next` in a `while` loop, parks on `Poll.Pending` via
##     `Thread.yield_now` (→ the reactor wait point), and stops on
##     `Poll.Ready(nil)` (stream end). NOT an `async def` and NOT `.await`-in-
##     loop (both gated): a hand pump like `block_on` / `Scope.collect`. So the
##     v1 consumer driver is `stream.for_each do |x| … end` from SYNC code, NOT
##     the spec sketch's `for x in stream` (which needs method-level async
##     lowering — deferred to v2; the ergonomic-`for-in` form is the follow-up).
##   * **v1 element channel is `Int`** (`Poll[Option[Int]]`) — the established
##     Task/Race/Scope/Queue convention; typed-generic `Stream[T]` deferred.
##   * v1 = single-consumer pull streams with leak-free cancel. NOT in scope:
##     concurrent stages (`mapPar`/`merge`), reactive-streams demand-protocol
##     interop — explicitly v2.
##
## `type Item` is declared for documentation/symmetry with `Enumerable[T]`; v1
## fixes the wire channel at `Int` (the `Poll[Option[Int]]` in the signatures),
## so adapters and the driver speak `Int` regardless of `Item`.
mixin Stream dispatch runtime
  type Item

  ## The ONE required primitive. Ready(Some(v)) = next element; Ready(nil) =
  ## end of stream; Pending = no element yet, parked (the source registered the
  ## waker on the reactor). Each source/adapter implements this.
  def var poll_next(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Option[Int]]

  ## SYNC consumer driver: pump `poll_next` to exhaustion, calling `f` on each
  ## element. Builds a `Context` (installs/holds the per-thread reactor for the
  ## drive). The `while`-with-`match` SYNC pump mirrors the block_on inline loop
  ## and `Scope.collect` — no `.await`, so it is not gated by the `.await`-in-
  ## loop `segment_cfg` set.
  ##
  ## On `Poll.Pending` it uses `Scope.pump_or_park` (NOT a bare
  ## `Thread.yield_now`): pump the thread-local scheduler once, and only PARK on
  ## the reactor if that made no progress AND no task is ready. Without the pump
  ## a stream whose source returns Pending until a SPAWNED task / Queue producer
  ## completes would never drive that task → spin forever (Codex high finding;
  ## same progress rule block_on and `Scope.collect` use). A reactor-backed
  ## source (timer/fd) parks correctly; a task-backed source gets pumped.
  ##
  ## Context lifecycle: v1 leaks ONE Context heap block per `for_each` drive
  ## (the same bounded leak class block_on has — the per-thread reactor is
  ## intentionally NOT released, and a `cx` local bound from `Context.executor`
  ## (a static-method result) is tainted out of drop-elaboration so no
  ## `ruxen_dealloc` fires). Bounded by the number of drains, not iterations or
  ## lifetime; documented, not hidden. A `Context`-reuse / explicit-teardown
  ## driver is a v2 follow-up.
  def var for_each(f: any Fn[Fn(Int) -> nil]) -> nil
    var cx = Context.executor
    var running = true
    while running
      match (&var self).poll_next(&var cx)
        Poll.Ready(item) ->
          match item
            Some(v) -> f.(v)
            nil     -> running = false
          end
        Poll.Pending -> Scope.pump_or_park
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  ## Drain the stream and return how many elements it yielded. A default body
  ## over `for_each` (which is itself over `poll_next`) — the `Enumerable`
  ## reuse shape.
  def var count -> Int
    var n = 0
    self.for_each do |x|
      n = n + 1
    end
    n
  end

  ## Drain the stream and return the SUM of its elements (v1 Int channel).
  ## Another `for_each`-derived default body; useful for asserting the exact
  ## element sequence in one number.
  def var sum -> Int
    var total = 0
    self.for_each do |x|
      total = total + x
    end
    total
  end
end

## ── Adapters ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
##
## Each adapter `include`s `Stream`, OWNS its inner stream BY VALUE as an
## `any Stream` (dyn-mixin) field, and implements `poll_next` by polling the
## inner via the runtime vtable (`(&var self.inner).poll_next(cx)`) and
## transforming. Dropping an adapter chain drops every stage (each runs its own
## `def drop`) — leak-free teardown by ownership.
##
## v1 SURFACE NOTE — adapters are constructed DIRECTLY (`MapStream.new(src, f)`,
## `FilterStream.new(src, pred)`, `TakeStream.new(src, n)`), NOT via fluent
## methods (`src.map(f)`). The spec sketch's `stream.map(f) -> MapStream[Self,U]`
## needs the method to MOVE `self` (the source) into the adapter — but moving
## `self` out of a `&var self` method body isn't supported (the explicit `(self)`
## consume form is `lib`-block-only). So the v1 surface takes the inner stream as
## a constructor ARG by value (the `Race.of`/`Queue` value-construction shape).
## The fluent `.map`/`.filter`/`.take` form is a v2 follow-up (by-move-self).

## `map` — apply `f` to each element. The output element is `f.(v)` (v1 Int→Int).
##
## A `done` latch FUSES the adapter: once the inner returns `nil` (end), every
## later `poll_next` returns `nil` WITHOUT re-polling the inner — so a non-fused
## inner that yields a value after its `nil` can't leak past the end (Codex
## medium finding). `for_each` already stops on the first `nil`, but `poll_next`
## is the public primitive and a composing adapter / custom driver may re-poll.
class MapStream
  inner: any Stream
  f: any Fn[Fn(Int) -> Int]
  done: Int
  include Stream
  type Item = Int

  def init(inner: any Stream, f: any Fn[Fn(Int) -> Int])
    self.inner = inner
    self.f = f
    self.done = 0
  end

  def var poll_next(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Option[Int]]
    if self.done != 0
      return Poll.Ready(nil)
    end
    match (&var self.inner).poll_next(cx)
      Poll.Ready(item) ->
        match item
          Some(v) -> Poll.Ready(Some(self.f.(v)))
          nil ->
            self.done = 1
            Poll.Ready(nil)
        end
      Poll.Pending -> Poll.Pending
    end
  end

  ## Drop the owned inner stream (runs ITS `def drop` via `Drop_dynamic_drop`,
  ## then frees its block) — so dropping a chain drops every stage (§4.5 Inc4).
  ## `self.inner` (an `any Stream` heap pointer) flows into the `Int`-typed
  ## helper at the i64 ABI (the `Task.spawn_raw`/`Race` future-value precedent);
  ## the helper null-guards, so no `.rx`-side null check is needed.
  def drop
    ruxen_stream_drop_inner(self.inner)
  end
end

## `filter` — keep only elements where `pred` holds. A dropped element does NOT
## end the stream — we must KEEP POLLING the inner until it yields a kept element
## or ends. The inner loop drains rejected elements in one `poll_next` call (so a
## reactor-backed inner that returns Pending mid-drain still surfaces Pending to
## the driver, which parks correctly).
class FilterStream
  inner: any Stream
  pred: any Fn[Fn(Int) -> Bool]
  done: Int
  include Stream
  type Item = Int

  def init(inner: any Stream, pred: any Fn[Fn(Int) -> Bool])
    self.inner = inner
    self.pred = pred
    self.done = 0
  end

  def var poll_next(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Option[Int]]
    if self.done != 0
      return Poll.Ready(nil)
    end
    var looking = true
    var out: Poll[Option[Int]] = Poll.Pending
    while looking
      match (&var self.inner).poll_next(cx)
        Poll.Ready(item) ->
          match item
            Some(v) ->
              if self.pred.(v)
                out = Poll.Ready(Some(v))
                looking = false
              end
            nil ->
              self.done = 1
              out = Poll.Ready(nil)
              looking = false
          end
        Poll.Pending ->
          out = Poll.Pending
          looking = false
      end
    end
    out
  end

  ## Drop the owned inner stream (chain teardown, §4.5 Inc4).
  def drop
    ruxen_stream_drop_inner(self.inner)
  end
end

## `take` — yield at most the first `remaining` elements, then end. Tearing the
## stream off early (dropping the TakeStream) drops the inner too.
class TakeStream
  inner: any Stream
  remaining: Int
  done: Int
  include Stream
  type Item = Int

  def init(inner: any Stream, n: Int)
    self.inner = inner
    self.remaining = n
    self.done = 0
  end

  def var poll_next(cx: &var Context) -> Poll[Option[Int]]
    if self.done != 0 || self.remaining <= 0
      self.done = 1
      return Poll.Ready(nil)
    end
    match (&var self.inner).poll_next(cx)
      Poll.Ready(item) ->
        match item
          Some(v) ->
            self.remaining = self.remaining - 1
            Poll.Ready(Some(v))
          nil ->
            # Inner ended before the quota — fuse so we don't re-poll it.
            self.done = 1
            Poll.Ready(nil)
        end
      Poll.Pending -> Poll.Pending
    end
  end

  ## Drop the owned inner stream (chain teardown, §4.5 Inc4).
  def drop
    ruxen_stream_drop_inner(self.inner)
  end
end

## §4.5 Increment 4 — adapter inner-drop helper (defined in runtime/stream.c).
## Runs the inner `any Stream`'s `def drop` via the compiler-synthesised
## `Drop_dynamic_drop` (the §4.1 path, now also emitted for Stream classes —
## the Drop-vtable gate was widened to `Future` OR `Stream`), then frees the
## inner's heap block. `self.inner` flows in as its i64 heap pointer.
lib "runtime/stream.c"
  def ruxen_stream_drop_inner(inner_ptr: Int) -> nil
end
